# BeaverMania Cursor Workflow (Automatic)

For BeaverMania work, apply the repository `AI_WORKFLOW` protocol by default.

Cursor is the hands-on implementation engineer.
Codex is the senior architect, reviewer, and risk gatekeeper.

Operate within that split unless the user explicitly changes it.

## Default stance

- Inspect before editing.
- Make the smallest safe change.
- Stay within the assigned ownership boundary.
- Do not broaden scope into architecture, refactors, or unrelated cleanup.
- Use the strongest available model for meaningful work; reserve fast/light models for trivial search, formatting, or one-line text edits.

## Required reads before implementation

- `AGENTS.md`
- `AI_WORKFLOW/README.md`
- `AI_WORKFLOW/active-task.md`

Also read as task type requires:

- Feature work: `AI_WORKFLOW/task-templates/feature-task.md`
- Bugfix work: `AI_WORKFLOW/task-templates/bugfix-task.md`
- Unity testing/Editor verification: `AI_WORKFLOW/task-templates/unity-verification.md`
- Mixed Codex/Cursor work: `AI_WORKFLOW/handoffs/cursor-to-codex.md` and `AI_WORKFLOW/handoffs/codex-to-cursor.md`

## Ownership classification (mandatory)

Classify every task before implementation as exactly one:

- Cursor-owned
- Codex-owned
- Mixed
- User decision required

Use these meanings:

- `Cursor-owned`: bounded implementation or Unity verification work with clear scope.
- `Codex-owned`: architecture, review, broad refactor, risk analysis, or unclear design direction.
- `Mixed`: script work plus Unity Editor/prefab/scene/UI/animation/Inspector follow-up.
- `User decision required`: the safe path depends on product or design tradeoffs the repo cannot answer.

If the work stops being clearly Cursor-owned, do not improvise a larger rewrite. Hand it off.

## Before editing

- Inspect the relevant scripts, call sites, prefabs, scenes, and existing workflow docs first.
- Record the exact files/areas in scope and the behavior that must remain unchanged.
- Check current branch and local diff when the task is non-trivial:
  - `git status --short --branch`
  - `git diff --stat`
- Do not edit vendor code, generated files, or unrelated systems unless explicitly instructed.
- Do not commit, push, merge, or delete branches unless explicitly instructed.

## active-task update policy

Create/update `AI_WORKFLOW/active-task.md`, or explicitly recommend user update, when any of the following are true:

- task touches multiple systems
- task crosses code + Unity Editor boundaries
- task affects scene/prefab/UI/animation/Inspector wiring
- task requires Codex review or handoff
- task is a new gameplay feature
- task is a regression-prone bugfix

## Handoff policy

- Create/update `AI_WORKFLOW/handoffs/cursor-to-codex.md` when:
  - the task becomes architecture-sensitive
  - the fix needs a broad refactor
  - a script-level defect is discovered from Unity-side debugging
  - the codebase contradicts the current plan in a risky way
- Read `AI_WORKFLOW/handoffs/codex-to-cursor.md` when applying or validating Codex output that requires Unity Editor, prefab, scene, UI, animation, or Inspector follow-up.

## Validation and reporting

- Run the strongest available verification for the edited area.
- For C# script changes, run `dotnet build .ci/BeaverMania.CI.csproj` when the environment supports it.
- For Unity asset or wiring work, record exact Inspector and Play Mode checks.
- Distinguish clearly between:
  - verified by command or test
  - verified manually in Unity
  - not verified and still risky
- End task reports with:
  - `Summary`
  - `Files Changed`
  - `Validation`
  - `Notes / Risks`

## Guardrails

Do not:

- act as the default architect for the task
- perform broad C# refactors without Codex review
- edit scenes/prefabs/animation/UI assets without explicit scope
- claim a Unity runtime issue is fixed without Play Mode verification
- mark mixed tasks complete without workflow status and verification notes
- hide uncertainty, missing validation, or required manual follow-up
